1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,940
We build agentic workflows by taking building blocks and putting them together to sequence out these complex workflows.

2
00:00:07,580 --> 00:00:17,720
In this video, I'd like to share with you a few of the key design patterns, which are patterns for how you can think about combining these building blocks into more complex workflows.

3
00:00:18,160 --> 00:00:18,800
Let's take a look.

4
00:00:19,120 --> 00:00:26,040
I think four key design patterns for building agentic workflows are perfection, tool use, planning, and multi-aging collaboration.

5
00:00:26,040 --> 00:00:33,220
Let me briefly go over what they mean, and then we'll actually go through most of these in-depth latent discourse as well.

6
00:00:33,420 --> 00:00:36,900
The first of the major design patterns is reflection.

7
00:00:37,560 --> 00:00:44,980
So I might go to an LLM agent and ask you to write code, and it turns out that an LLM might then generate code like this.

8
00:00:45,100 --> 00:00:47,820
It defines here a Python function to do a certain task.

9
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:50,460
I could then construct a prompt that looks like this.

10
00:00:50,560 --> 00:00:53,220
I can say, here's code intended for a certain task,

11
00:00:53,220 --> 00:00:56,900
and then copy-paste whatever the LLM had just output back into this prompt.

12
00:00:57,180 --> 00:00:58,840
And then I'm going to ask it to check the code carefully,

13
00:00:58,980 --> 00:01:01,580
to correct the style and efficiency, and give constructive criticism.

14
00:01:01,860 --> 00:01:07,660
And it turns out that the same LLM model prompted this way may be able to point out some problems with the code.

15
00:01:07,820 --> 00:01:14,340
And if I then take this critique and feed it back to the model to say, looks like this is a bug.

16
00:01:14,500 --> 00:01:16,100
Could you change the code to fix it?

17
00:01:16,200 --> 00:01:19,920
Then it may actually come with a better version of the code.

18
00:01:19,920 --> 00:01:26,300
To give a preview of tool use, if you're able to run the code and see where the code fails,

19
00:01:27,060 --> 00:01:33,780
then feeding that back to the LLM can also cause it to be able to iterate and generate a much better,

20
00:01:33,840 --> 00:01:36,060
say V3 version 3 of the code.

21
00:01:36,460 --> 00:01:41,420
So reflection is a common design pattern where you can ask the LLM to examine its own outputs,

22
00:01:41,420 --> 00:01:48,180
or maybe bring in some external sources of information such as run the code and see if it generates any error messages,

23
00:01:48,780 --> 00:01:53,820
and use that as feedback to iterate again and come over a better version of its output.

24
00:01:54,180 --> 00:01:59,680
And this design pattern isn't magic. It does not result in everything working 100% of the time.

25
00:01:59,820 --> 00:02:03,360
But sometimes it can be a nice bump in the performance of your system.

26
00:02:03,360 --> 00:02:07,400
Now I've drawn this as if it was a single LLM that I'm prompting,

27
00:02:07,580 --> 00:02:10,220
but to foreshadow multi-agent workflows,

28
00:02:10,580 --> 00:02:14,620
you can also imagine instead of having the same model critique itself,

29
00:02:14,900 --> 00:02:16,880
you can imagine having a critic agent.

30
00:02:17,040 --> 00:02:20,980
And all that is, is an LLM that's been prompted with instructions like

31
00:02:20,980 --> 00:02:22,660
your role is to critique code,

32
00:02:22,800 --> 00:02:25,760
here's code intended for a task, check the code carefully and so on.

33
00:02:25,760 --> 00:02:29,860
and the second critic agent, maybe point out errors or run unit tests.

34
00:02:30,160 --> 00:02:34,800
And by having two simulated agents where each agent is just an LLM

35
00:02:34,800 --> 00:02:36,760
prompted to take on a certain persona,

36
00:02:37,040 --> 00:02:40,820
you can have them go back and forth to iterate to get a better output.

37
00:02:41,380 --> 00:02:46,360
In addition to reflection pattern, the second important design pattern is tool use,

38
00:02:46,660 --> 00:02:52,560
where today LLMs can be given tools, meaning functions that they can call in order to get work done.

39
00:02:52,560 --> 00:02:55,680
For example, if you ask an LLM, what's the best coffee maker,

40
00:02:55,760 --> 00:02:58,240
according to reviewers, and you give it a web search tool,

41
00:02:58,400 --> 00:03:02,100
then it can actually search the internet to find much better answers.

42
00:03:02,440 --> 00:03:03,780
Or a code execution tool.

43
00:03:04,060 --> 00:03:06,680
If you ask a math question, like if I invest $100,

44
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:08,580
a compound interest, what have you in,

45
00:03:08,760 --> 00:03:12,240
it can then write code and execute code to compute an answer.

46
00:03:12,520 --> 00:03:15,800
Today, different developers have given LLMs many different tools

47
00:03:15,800 --> 00:03:19,640
for everything from math or data analysis to gather information

48
00:03:19,640 --> 00:03:22,200
by fetching things from the web or from various databases,

49
00:03:22,200 --> 00:03:26,060
to interface with productivity apps like email, calendar and so on,

50
00:03:26,260 --> 00:03:29,340
as well as to process images and much more.

51
00:03:29,500 --> 00:03:33,380
And the ability of an LLM to decide what tools to use,

52
00:03:33,560 --> 00:03:37,640
meaning what functions to call, that lets the model get a lot more done.

53
00:03:37,880 --> 00:03:41,140
The third of the four design patterns is planning.

54
00:03:41,500 --> 00:03:45,300
This is an example from a paper called Hugging GPT,

55
00:03:45,300 --> 00:03:51,440
in which if you ask a system to please generate an image where a girl is reading a book

56
00:03:51,440 --> 00:03:53,160
and a post is the same as a boy in the image,

57
00:03:53,260 --> 00:03:55,300
then please describe the new image in your voice,

58
00:03:55,540 --> 00:03:59,340
then a model can automatically decide that to carry out this task,

59
00:03:59,600 --> 00:04:04,400
it first needs to find a post-determination model to figure out the post of the boy,

60
00:04:04,540 --> 00:04:08,080
then to post the image to generate a picture of a girl and an image to text,

61
00:04:08,080 --> 00:04:12,880
and then finally text-to-speech. And so in planning an LLM decides

62
00:04:12,880 --> 00:04:15,940
what is the sequence of actions it needs to take.

63
00:04:16,020 --> 00:04:19,520
In this case, it is a sequence of API calls,

64
00:04:19,560 --> 00:04:23,840
so that it can then carry out the right sequence of steps in the right order

65
00:04:23,840 --> 00:04:25,980
in order to carry out the task.

66
00:04:25,980 --> 00:04:30,100
So rather than the developer hard coding the sequence of steps in advance,

67
00:04:30,400 --> 00:04:34,100
this actually lets the LLM decide what are the steps to take.

68
00:04:34,300 --> 00:04:38,240
Agents that plan today are harder to control and somewhat more experimental,

69
00:04:38,800 --> 00:04:41,280
but sometimes it can give really delightful results.

70
00:04:41,680 --> 00:04:44,040
And then finally, multi-agent workflows.

71
00:04:44,380 --> 00:04:49,780
Just as a human manager might hire a number of others to work together on a complex project,

72
00:04:50,020 --> 00:04:54,280
in some cases, it might make sense for you to hire a set of multiple agents,

73
00:04:54,280 --> 00:04:57,120
maybe each of which specializes in a different role,

74
00:04:57,280 --> 00:05:00,880
and have them work together to accomplish a complex task.

75
00:05:01,040 --> 00:05:05,160
The picture you see here on the left is taken from a project called ChatDev,

76
00:05:05,280 --> 00:05:09,500
which is a software framework created by Chen Qian and collaborators.

77
00:05:09,820 --> 00:05:12,380
In ChatDev, multiple agents with different roles,

78
00:05:12,700 --> 00:05:17,060
like Chief Executive Officer, Programmer, Tester, Designer, and so on,

79
00:05:17,240 --> 00:05:21,100
collaborate together as if they were a virtual software company,

80
00:05:21,100 --> 00:05:26,140
and can collaboratively complete a range of software development tasks.

81
00:05:26,340 --> 00:05:27,820
Let's consider another example.

82
00:05:28,040 --> 00:05:30,320
If you want to write a marketing brochure,

83
00:05:30,560 --> 00:05:33,540
maybe you'd think of hiring a team of three people,

84
00:05:33,740 --> 00:05:36,520
such as a researcher to do online research,

85
00:05:36,720 --> 00:05:38,560
a marketer to write the marketing text,

86
00:05:38,780 --> 00:05:42,400
and then finally an editor to edit and polish the text.

87
00:05:42,640 --> 00:05:44,300
And so, in a similar way,

88
00:05:44,460 --> 00:05:47,920
you might consider building a multi-agent workflow,

89
00:05:47,920 --> 00:05:50,320
in which you have a simulated research agent,

90
00:05:50,640 --> 00:05:52,320
a simulated marketer agent,

91
00:05:52,560 --> 00:05:54,170
and a simulated editor agent,

92
00:05:54,780 --> 00:05:58,300
that then come together to carry out this task for you.

93
00:05:58,640 --> 00:06:01,720
Multi-agent workflows are more difficult to control,

94
00:06:01,980 --> 00:06:05,280
since you don't always know ahead of time what the agents will do,

95
00:06:05,460 --> 00:06:08,640
but research has shown that they can result in better outcomes

96
00:06:08,640 --> 00:06:10,360
for many complex tasks,

97
00:06:10,760 --> 00:06:13,100
including things like writing biographies

98
00:06:13,100 --> 00:06:15,200
or deciding on chess moves to make a game.

99
00:06:15,200 --> 00:06:18,980
You'll learn more about multi-agentic workflows later in this course as well.

100
00:06:19,400 --> 00:06:23,500
And so with that, I hope you have a sense of what agentic workflows can do,

101
00:06:23,880 --> 00:06:27,580
as well as of what are the key challenges of finding building blocks

102
00:06:27,580 --> 00:06:28,740
and putting them together,

103
00:06:28,880 --> 00:06:30,780
maybe via these design patterns,

104
00:06:31,120 --> 00:06:33,320
in order to implement an agentic workflow.

105
00:06:33,800 --> 00:06:35,940
And of course, also developing evals

106
00:06:35,940 --> 00:06:37,620
so you can see how well your system is doing

107
00:06:37,620 --> 00:06:39,720
and keep on improving on it.

108
00:06:39,720 --> 00:06:42,020
In the next module,

109
00:06:42,340 --> 00:06:43,780
I'd like to share with you

110
00:06:43,780 --> 00:06:47,380
a deep dive into the first of these design patterns,

111
00:06:47,740 --> 00:06:48,880
that is reflection,

112
00:06:49,260 --> 00:06:50,420
and you'll find that

113
00:06:50,420 --> 00:06:53,780
it may be a surprisingly simple to implement technique

114
00:06:53,780 --> 00:06:55,900
that can give the performance of your system

115
00:06:55,900 --> 00:06:58,160
sometimes a very nice bump.

116
00:06:58,520 --> 00:07:00,760
So let's go on to the next module

117
00:07:00,760 --> 00:07:03,820
to learn about the reflection design pattern.

118
00:07:03,820 --> 00:07:03,900
.

